@node Install from source
@section Install from source

@cindex install from source

Clone the repository and add it to the load path.

@lisp
(add-to-list 'load-path "~/path/to/latex-structure")
(require 'latex-structure)
(add-hook 'LaTeX-mode-hook #'latex-structure-mode) ; AUCTeX
(add-hook 'latex-mode-hook #'latex-structure-mode) ; built-in mode
@end lisp

With @code{use-package} and a local checkout:

@lisp
(use-package latex-structure
  :load-path "~/path/to/latex-structure"
  :hook ((LaTeX-mode latex-mode) . latex-structure-mode))
@end lisp

@node Install from MELPA
@section Install from MELPA

@cindex MELPA

When the package is on MELPA, install it with @code{M-x package-install
RET latex-structure RET} and add the hooks above without the
@code{load-path} line.

@node Requirements
@section Requirements

@cindex requirements

The package needs Emacs 28.1 or newer.  AUC@TeX{} is optional.  The mode
line shows @samp{Struct} when @code{latex-structure-mode} is on.

@node Getting started
@chapter Getting started

@cindex getting started
@cindex tutorial

Copy this small document into a buffer and turn on
@code{latex-structure-mode}.

@example
\documentclass@{book@}
\begin@{document@}
\chapter@{Introduction@}
\section@{Alpha@}
Alpha text.
\section@{Beta@}
Beta text.
\begin@{itemize@}
\item one
\item two
\item three
\end@{itemize@}
\chapter@{Methods@}
Methods text.
\end@{document@}
@end example

@enumerate
@item
Put point anywhere on the line @samp{\section@{Beta@}} and press
@code{M-<up>}.  Beta moves above Alpha.  Press @code{M-<down>} to move it
back.
@item
On the same line press @code{M-<right>}.  The heading becomes
@samp{\subsection@{Beta@}}.  Press @code{M-<left>} to promote it again.
@item
Press @code{C-c C-w}.  The minibuffer offers targets such as
@samp{*scratch*: Methods}.  Choose the Methods target.  Beta and its
list move to the end of the Methods chapter.
@item
Put point on @samp{\item two} and press @code{M-<right>}.  The item moves
into a new nested @code{itemize} inside @samp{\item one}.  Press @code{M-<left>}
to move it back out.
@item
Press @code{C-c -} inside the list.  The list becomes @code{enumerate}.
@item
Press @code{C-c /} and type @samp{text}.  Only the matching lines and the
headings above them stay visible.  Run @code{M-x
   latex-structure-sparse-tree-remove} to show everything again.
@end enumerate

@node Sections
@chapter Sections

@cindex sections
@cindex headings

A heading is a line that starts, after optional indentation and an
optional comment prefix, with a command listed in
@code{latex-structure-section-levels}.  The default list is @code{\part},
@code{\chapter}, @code{\section}, @code{\subsection}, @code{\subsubsection}, @code{\paragraph},
and @code{\subparagraph}.  Starred forms and optional short titles count.

A section starts at its heading and runs to the next heading of the
same or a higher rank.  Its subsections travel with it.

@menu
* Section boundaries::
* Moving, promoting, and demoting: Moving promoting and demoting. 
@end menu

@node Section boundaries
@section Section boundaries

@cindex section boundaries
@cindex \include, as a boundary
@vindex latex-structure-section-boundary-regexp

Some lines end every section that comes before them, whatever the
level.  The defaults are @samp{\end@{document@}}, @samp{\appendix},
@samp{\frontmatter}, @samp{\mainmatter}, @samp{\backmatter}, @samp{\bibliography@{...@}},
@samp{\printbibliography}, and @samp{\include@{...@}}.  Because of this, moving
or refiling the last section of a master file never carries the
bibliography, the end of the document, or the @code{\include} lines of
the other chapters along with it.  Change the list with
@code{latex-structure-section-boundary-regexp}.

@node Moving promoting and demoting
@section Moving, promoting, and demoting

@cindex moving sections
@cindex promoting headings
@cindex demoting headings
@findex latex-structure-move-section-up
@findex latex-structure-move-section-down
@findex latex-structure-promote-heading
@findex latex-structure-demote-heading
@findex latex-structure-promote-section
@findex latex-structure-demote-section

These commands act when point is anywhere on a heading line.

@itemize
@item
@code{M-<up>} and @code{M-<down>} swap the section with its previous or next
sibling.  A section cannot move past its parent.
@item
@code{M-<left>} and @code{M-<right>} promote or demote the heading line only.
@item
@code{M-S-<left>} and @code{M-S-<right>} promote or demote the heading and
every heading inside the section.
@end itemize

A change of level is all or nothing.  When any heading in the section
would leave the range of @code{latex-structure-section-levels}, nothing
changes and an error explains why.

@node Refiling across a document
@chapter Refiling across a document

@cindex refiling
@cindex multi-file documents
@findex latex-structure-refile
@findex latex-structure-refile-copy

@code{C-c C-w} runs @code{latex-structure-refile}.  It moves the section around
point under another heading, in the same file or in another file of
the document.  @code{C-c M-w} runs @code{latex-structure-refile-copy}, which
leaves the original section in place.

@menu
* Which files take part::
* Targets::
* Where the section lands::
* Jumping to a heading::
@end menu

@node Which files take part
@section Which files take part

@cindex master file
@cindex TeX-master
@findex latex-structure-project-files
@vindex latex-structure-include-regexp

The package finds the master file in this order.

@enumerate
@item
The value of @code{TeX-master}, when AUC@TeX{} has set it to a file name.
@item
The value of @code{tex-main-file} in the built-in mode.
@item
The file of the current buffer.
@end enumerate

From the master file it follows @code{\input}, @code{\include}, @code{\subfile},
@code{\import}, and @code{\subimport} in document order.  Names in @code{\input} and
@code{\include} are relative to the directory of the master file, as in
@LaTeX{}.  A missing @code{.tex} extension is added.  Commented-out lines are
skipped.  The buffer of a file that is open counts with its unsaved
edits.  Change the include commands with
@code{latex-structure-include-regexp}.

@node Targets
@section Targets

@cindex refile targets
@vindex latex-structure-refile-include-commented

Each target shows the file name and the path of titles down to the
heading, for example @samp{chapters/ch02.tex: Methods / Sample preparation}.
Each file also offers a target named @samp{ch02.tex (top level)}.  Two
headings with the same path get a suffix such as @samp{<2>}.
Commented-out headings are left out, unless
@code{latex-structure-refile-include-commented} is non-nil.

@node Where the section lands
@section Where the section lands

@cindex heading levels, refile
@vindex latex-structure-refile-adjust-levels

The section becomes the last child of the target heading.  When
@code{latex-structure-refile-adjust-levels} is non-nil, which is the
default, its headings change rank to fit.  A @code{\section} refiled under
another @code{\section} becomes a @code{\subsection}, and its subsections move
down one rank too.  A section refiled under a @code{\chapter} stays a
@code{\section}.

A section refiled to the top level of a file keeps its levels.  It
lands after the last section of the file and before
@samp{\end@{document@}} or the bibliography.

Refiling into another file visits that file and leaves it modified
but unsaved, so you can review the change before you save.  A section
cannot be refiled into itself or into one of its own subsections.

@node Jumping to a heading
@section Jumping to a heading

@cindex goto
@findex latex-structure-goto

@code{C-u C-c C-w} offers the same targets but only jumps to the chosen
heading.  This is the analog of @code{org-goto} and of @code{org-refile} with a
prefix argument.  The command @code{latex-structure-goto} does the same
thing.  The mark is set before the jump, so @code{C-u C-SPC} returns.

@node Indirect buffers
@chapter Indirect buffers

@cindex indirect buffer
@findex latex-structure-tree-to-indirect-buffer
@vindex latex-structure-indirect-buffer-display

@code{C-c C-x b} runs @code{latex-structure-tree-to-indirect-buffer}.  It
shows the current section alone in an indirect buffer named after
the file and the section title.  Edits there change the original
buffer.  The command reuses one indirect buffer and kills the
previous one, unless you give it a prefix argument.

Choose where the buffer appears with
@code{latex-structure-indirect-buffer-display}.  The values are
@code{other-window}, @code{current-window}, and @code{new-frame}.

@node Sparse trees
@chapter Sparse trees

@cindex sparse tree
@findex latex-structure-sparse-tree
@findex latex-structure-sparse-tree-remove

@code{C-c /} runs @code{latex-structure-sparse-tree}.  It asks for a regexp and
hides every line except the matching lines and the headings above
them.  The matches are highlighted with the face
@code{latex-structure-sparse-match}.  An ellipsis marks each hidden
stretch.  Isearch opens a hidden stretch when a match lies inside it.

Show the whole buffer again with @code{latex-structure-sparse-tree-remove},
by calling @code{C-c /} with an empty regexp, or by turning the mode off.

@node List items
@chapter List items

@cindex list items
@cindex itemize, enumerate, description
@vindex latex-structure-list-environments

An item starts at @code{\item} and runs to the next @code{\item} of the same
list or to the @code{\end} of the list.  Nested lists inside an item
belong to that item and travel with it.  Commands such as @code{\itemsep}
are not items.  The list environments are those in
@code{latex-structure-list-environments}.  Add enumitem clones or your own
list environments there.

@menu
* Moving items::
* Indenting and outdenting::
* Changing the list type::
@end menu

@node Moving items
@section Moving items

@cindex moving items
@findex latex-structure-move-item-up
@findex latex-structure-move-item-down
@findex latex-structure-next-item
@findex latex-structure-previous-item

Inside an item, @code{M-<up>} and @code{M-<down>} swap it with the previous or
next item of the same list.  Point stays at the same place inside
the moved item.  The commands @code{latex-structure-next-item} and
@code{latex-structure-previous-item} move point between items.

@node Indenting and outdenting
@section Indenting and outdenting

@cindex indenting items
@cindex outdenting items
@findex latex-structure-indent-item
@findex latex-structure-outdent-item
@vindex latex-structure-reindent

@code{M-<right>} makes the item the last child of the previous item.  When
the previous item already ends with a nested list, the item joins
that list.  Otherwise a new nested list of the same type is made.
The first item of a list cannot be indented.

@code{M-<left>} makes a nested item the sibling that follows its parent
item.  When it was the only item of its nested list, the empty list
is removed.  An item of a top-level list cannot be outdented.

After an indent or outdent the list is reindented with the
indentation rules of the major mode.  Set @code{latex-structure-reindent}
to nil to keep the text as it is.

@node Changing the list type
@section Changing the list type

@cindex list type
@findex latex-structure-cycle-list-type
@vindex latex-structure-list-cycle

@code{C-c -} changes the innermost list around point to the next type in
@code{latex-structure-list-cycle}.  The default cycle is @code{itemize},
@code{enumerate}, @code{description}, and back to @code{itemize}.  With @code{C-u} the
command asks for the type.  Both the @code{\begin} and the @code{\end} line
change.  Labels of @code{description} items are not added or removed.

@node Key bindings
@chapter Key bindings

@cindex key bindings
@cindex conditional keys
@findex latex-structure-mode

The Meta-arrow keys and @code{C-c -} are conditional.  They act only on a
heading line or in a list, and elsewhere they keep their usual
meaning, so @code{M-<left>} still moves back one word in body text.

@float Table,org16d14c2
