@tab @code{latex-structure-sparse-tree}
@end multitable
@caption{Keys of latex-structure-mode that act anywhere.}
@end float

To use other keys, change @code{latex-structure-mode-map}.

@lisp
(with-eval-after-load 'latex-structure
  (define-key latex-structure-mode-map (kbd "C-c C-w") nil)
  (define-key latex-structure-mode-map (kbd "C-c r") #'latex-structure-refile))
@end lisp

@node Speed commands
@chapter Speed commands

@cindex speed commands
@findex latex-structure-speed-commands-setup
@vindex latex-structure-speed-commands
@vindex latex-structure-item-speed-commands

When you also use @LaTeX{} Speed Commands, add single-key commands after
both packages load.

@lisp
(with-eval-after-load 'latex-speed-commands
  (require 'latex-structure)
  (latex-structure-speed-commands-setup))
@end lisp

The setup puts the entries of @code{latex-structure-speed-commands} at the
front of @code{latex-speed-commands}, so they win over entries with the
same key.  It also adds @code{latex-structure-item-speed-activate} to
@code{latex-speed-commands-activate-functions}, which makes the keys in
@code{latex-structure-item-speed-commands} active at the start of an
@code{\item} line.  The start is the beginning of the line or the
@code{\item} token after the indentation.  Calling the setup twice adds
nothing new.

@float Table,orga532e2c
@multitable {aaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem Key
@tab Command
@item @code{W}
@tab Refile the section
@item @code{g}
@tab Jump to any heading in the document
@item @code{T}
@tab Show the section in an indirect buffer
@item @code{/}
@tab Sparse tree, in place of @code{occur}
@end multitable
@caption{Speed keys at heading lines.}
@end float

@float Table,orgaa365a9
@multitable {aaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem Key
@tab Command
@item @code{n}
@tab Next item
@item @code{p}
@tab Previous item
@item @code{U}
@tab Move item up
@item @code{D}
@tab Move item down
@item @code{l}
@tab Outdent item
@item @code{r}
@tab Indent item
@item @code{-}
@tab Cycle the list type
@end multitable
@caption{Speed keys at the start of an \item line.}
@end float

Org uses @code{w} for refiling, but @LaTeX{} Speed Commands uses @code{w} to copy a
section.  That is why refiling is on @code{W}.

